WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.620
Permission enforcement is a core requirement for private RAG.

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:08.313
The system must not allow users to retrieve documents or chunks they

00:00:08.313 --> 00:00:12.224
could not access through the approved source system or workflow.

00:00:12.224 --> 00:00:16.136
This requirement applies even when the answer is summarized, transformed,

00:00:16.136 --> 00:00:18.482
or returned through a conversational interface.

00:00:18.482 --> 00:00:22.393
Enforcement can happen at ingestion time, query time, or both.

00:00:22.393 --> 00:00:26.304
Ingestion-time separation may reduce risk by placing different tenants, classifications,

00:00:26.304 --> 00:00:28.260
or departments into different collections.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:32.562
Query-time enforcement is often essential because user access changes, documents move,

00:00:32.562 --> 00:00:36.473
groups are updated, projects close, and workflow-specific restrictions may apply.

00:00:36.473 --> 00:00:41.167
A strong design connects user identity, group membership, service accounts, application roles,

00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:45.078
tenant IDs, document permissions, metadata filters, row-level or document-level rules,

00:00:45.078 --> 00:00:46.251
and audit trails.

00:00:46.251 --> 00:00:51.336
The retrieval service should know who is asking, what workflow they are using,

00:00:51.336 --> 00:00:55.638
and what content is eligible before selecting context for the model.

00:00:55.638 --> 00:00:57.593
Administrative access deserves separate treatment.

00:00:57.593 --> 00:01:00.331
People who manage indexes, prompts, connectors, logs,

00:01:00.331 --> 00:01:05.416
or source mappings may have visibility and influence that ordinary users do not.

00:01:05.416 --> 00:01:08.544
Admin roles should be narrow, reviewable, and logged.

00:01:08.544 --> 00:01:13.238
Private RAG should favor least privilege and deny-by-default behavior for restricted material.

00:01:13.238 --> 00:01:17.931
Do not rely only on separate user interfaces or broad shared indexes

00:01:17.931 --> 00:01:19.887
when sensitive content is involved.

00:01:19.887 --> 00:01:23.016
Test permission boundaries before launch, after source changes,

00:01:23.016 --> 00:01:25.362
and whenever the retrieval architecture changes.

00:01:25.362 --> 00:01:28.882
Access tests should use realistic roles and document examples.

00:01:28.882 --> 00:01:32.402
Include users with partial access, users from other tenants,

00:01:32.402 --> 00:01:34.749
users whose access was recently removed,

00:01:34.749 --> 00:01:38.660
and service identities that support background jobs or evaluation workflows.
